  <!-- ========== 3. DELTA (verb-disciplined, banned-verb lint enforced) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24&ndash;48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive &middot; banned-verb lint enforced &middot; each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">

      <p>The US-Iran negotiating track entered acute collapse between May 10 and May 13 as Trump characterised Iran&rsquo;s counter-proposal as &ldquo;a piece of garbage&rdquo; and declared the ceasefire &ldquo;on life support.&rdquo; Iran responded through two distinct registers: Pezeshkian&rsquo;s &ldquo;we will never bow&rdquo; defiance framing on May 11 and Araghchi&rsquo;s procedural &ldquo;US approach is the main obstacle&rdquo; formulation delivered in a bilateral session with Norway&rsquo;s Deputy FM on May 12. Neither statement named the US-authored 14-point framework by title or addressed its specific terms, keeping Iran inside a posture of principled non-engagement rather than explicit rejection. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>Iran&rsquo;s foreign minister travelled to the BRICS foreign ministers&rsquo; meeting in New Delhi on May 13&ndash;14, demonstrating Tehran&rsquo;s refusal to treat the collapsed Pakistan channel as the only available diplomatic arena. Araghchi&rsquo;s presence at the BRICS format &mdash; where he engaged with Jaishankar and other multilateral counterparts &mdash; directed diplomatic bandwidth toward frameworks where Iran holds a stronger structural position than in bilateral US-mediated channels. The Witkoff-Kushner Islamabad trip has been cancelled with no confirmed reschedule; the IRGC Quds Force has issued no statement endorsing the diplomatic track. No operational pathway exists for a signed written memorandum by May 18. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span> (0.35 → 0.07)</p>

      <p>The May 12&ndash;13 Jiyeh coastal highway strikes &mdash; three IDF drone attacks targeting vehicles approximately 20 kilometres south of Beirut &mdash; killed eight to fifteen people including two children. All tier-1 sourcing places the strikes within the Jiyeh area of Mount Lebanon governorate rather than Beirut governorate; IDF attributed the attacks to Hezbollah infrastructure interdiction without naming Beirut as a target. Hezbollah rocket fire stands confirmed above 40 per day, crossing the &gt;20/day indicator threshold. The geographic northward creep of IDF operations from southern Lebanon through the Jiyeh corridor raises the conditional probability of a strike within Beirut governorate boundaries before EOD May 15. The forecast&rsquo;s horizon expires tomorrow. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span> (0.30 → 0.40)</p>

      <p>Saudi Arabia and Kuwait restored US basing and overflight access to Prince Sultan Air Base following a second Trump-MBS phone call, per WSJ, Iran International, and Defense News reporting around May 7&ndash;8. The operational restoration is real; the MBS-Trump indicator is observed. However, the public-confirmation pathway &mdash; requiring Saudi MOFA or a named Pentagon spokesperson statement &mdash; faces a structural barrier that Saudi Arabia has historically sustained: the Pentagon referred press questions to the White House, and no MOFA statement has been issued in seventeen days since restoration. The YES-resolution path for F5 by May 25 requires an unusual departure from Saudi Arabia&rsquo;s documented basing communication practice. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span> (0.40 → 0.28)</p>

      <p>Trump arrived in Beijing on May 13&ndash;14 for a summit with Xi Jinping on May 14&ndash;15, with Iran&rsquo;s Hormuz closure on the central agenda. China purchases over 80% of Iranian crude and holds the relationship that most directly conditions Iranian economic sustainability, giving Xi leverage he did not anticipate entering 2026. Even if the summit produces Chinese engagement on the Hormuz file, a YES resolution for F6 by June 10 requires a near-linear chain &mdash; Chinese commitment, Iranian operational compliance, Lloyd&rsquo;s re-rating below 1.5%, and major shipping line announcements &mdash; at a moment when daily transits stand at 6&ndash;12 vessels against a 50-vessel threshold. A named Chinese Hormuz commitment would materially shift this probability; no such commitment had been issued as of this report&rsquo;s compilation. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span> (0.45 → 0.18)</p>

      <p>The Israeli coalition reached a structural inflection on May 12&ndash;13. Degel Hatorah announced departure from the coalition on May 12; the coalition then pulled all Knesset legislation citing lack of majority; Netanyahu&rsquo;s full coalition co-submitted a Knesset dissolution bill on May 13, framing a managed path to September elections. Per Codex DA #12, F7 carries weekly cadence and receives no intra-week probability delta today. The dissolution process creates conditions for an ambiguous interaction between F7 and N1: if the dissolution bill passes preliminary reading before May 28 (p=0.72), elections would occur via scheduled vote rather than coalition collapse, potentially resolving F7 as YES on the amended ambiguity rule (election date moved earlier than Oct 27) or requiring Sunday adjudication. → supports <span class="cite">F7</span> without move | → created <span class="cite">N1</span> (p=0.72)</p>

    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
